// Stone 53 fixture: a floor block inside another
// floor's handed values — a shape the language does
// not have. The Swift lexer reads a floor's values by
// collecting tokens itself, and a nested floor-with-
// values assigns the pending-token queue as it
// finishes, stranding the outer collection, which
// then reports its '(' as never closed. Both sides
// refuse this program with that very message; the
// fixture pins the refusal so the fact stands in the
// corpus, named, rather than surprising anyone later.
// It is a fact about the language, not a divergence:
// the port's lexer was corrected to this behaviour in
// Stone 47, and the translator is never reached.
let nested = floor(floor(3) {
    mov rax, [r10]
}) {
    mov rax, [r10]
}
say nested
